{"name":"Avocado Stone","hex":"#4e3e1f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"avocado","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The large, usually yellowish-green or black, savory fruit of the avocado tree.","The avocado tree, Persea americana, of the laurel family."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a dull yellowish-green colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/avocado"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Avocado","blocks":[{"text":"The avocado, alligator pear or avocado pear is an evergreen tree in the laurel family (Lauraceae). It is native to the Americas, with archaeological evidence of early human avocado use dating back thousands of years across various regions of Central and South America. It was prized for its large and unusually oily fruit. The native range of avocado extends from Mexico to Peru, encompassing much of Central America and parts of northern and western South America."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Persea_americana_fruit_2.JPG/330px-Persea_americana_fruit_2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Avocado","wikidata":"Q37153"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)","wikidata":"Q8063"},{"title":"Shades of chartreuse","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of chartreuse are listed below. Chartreuse is a color between yellow and green, so named because of its resemblance to the color of the French liqueur green chartreuse."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Shades_of_chartreuse_%28x25%29.svg/330px-Shades_of_chartreuse_%28x25%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_chartreuse","wikidata":"Q105563495"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Avocado_Hass_-_single_and_halved.jpg/500px-Avocado_Hass_-_single_and_halved.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Avocado_Hass_-_single_and_halved.jpg/1280px-Avocado_Hass_-_single_and_halved.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Avocado_Hass_-_single_and_halved.jpg/500px-Avocado_Hass_-_single_and_halved.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAvocado_Hass_-_single_and_halved.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ab/Flower_of_Avocado_2.jpg/500px-Flower_of_Avocado_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pa